{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_58","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\": \"submission.json\", \"summary\": \"First product of this run: a sourced partial map plus the first Candidate for the EPaxos replica in the captured swiftpaxos snapshot.\\n\\nWhat was done. I read the epaxos package (epaxos.go, exec.go, defs.go) and the shared helpers it depends on (replica/replica.go quorum sizes and listeners, replica/defs, state/state.go, run.go launch wiring), then traced both core lines end to end: command admission -> startPhase1 attribute derivation -> PreAccept on receivers -> reply merge -> fast-path commit or majority Accept -> Commit install, and ballot promise/raise (bal, vbal, maxRecvBallot, makeBallot) -> execution-timeout driven recovery -> prepare over a slow quorum -> highest-vbal value selection -> accept / try-preAccept / phase-1 restart, including how committed records short-circuit recovery, how lower-ballot messages are dropped, and how pending client proposals survive a leader change. The map records 7 activities, 16 behaviors, 7 facts, target profile, and 7 surfaced unread boundaries, with the overview marked usable and the anomalies kept as open_details rather than asserted as defects.\\n\\nFirst Candidate. handlePreAccept is the only handler that addresses an instance two ways: it reads and writes InstanceSpace[preAccept.Replica][Instance] at lines 782, 792-798, 810, 816 and 828-829, but at line 809 stores the received command into InstanceSpace[preAccept.LeaderId][Instance]. If any legal history reaches that branch (local Status >= ACCEPTED with nil Cmds) while LeaderId != Replica, the addressed record (Replica, Instance) is left command-less (executeCommands requires Cmds != nil, so its row halts) and an unrelated record (LeaderId, Instance) gains a command no quorum committed for it. The Candidate deliberately fixes the unresolved premise as its discriminator: reading found no path that produces Status >= ACCEPTED with nil Cmds (handleAccept stores bal/vbal/Seq/Deps but records no ACCEPTED status; leader-side transitions copy lb.cmds; a nil Command does not survive the wire format as nil), so reachability is the open question, not the discrepancy itself. Counterevidence and both interpretations are retained.\\n\\nOther sourced anomalies were recorded as open_details only (identical guards for handlePrepareReply sub-cases 3 and 4; the defer-cycle guard counting lb.possibleQuorum[tpar.AcceptorId] in a loop over q; the stable-store header writing bal and vbal into the same bytes with no reader; the inert transconf block; the N<=3 with Thrifty equality exemption). Local compilation of ./epaxos with the captured module cache succeeds, so a controlled in-package harness (replicas wired through in-memory peer writers) is feasible for the next step; no formal check was submitted this turn.\"}"}}
